EXT. DUST STORM - FRONTIER MAIN STREET - DAY

Wind. Grit. A hanging sign banging. Visibility ten feet.

A figure in a duster walks down the center of the street. Head down. Hand on a holster. This is SHERIFF AUGUSTA PIKE, 40s.

She stops outside the saloon.

She listens.

Inside the saloon, a piano plays one bar. Stops.

She pushes through the swinging doors.

INT. SALOON - CONTINUOUS

Dim. Empty tables. A man at the bar, his back to her. Black hat. Long coat. He does not turn.

She steps in. Boots on boards. Slow.

He lifts a glass. Drinks.

SHERIFF PIKE
Mr. Crane.

CRANE
Sheriff.

SHERIFF PIKE
You're a long way from Laramie.

CRANE
He's longer.

SHERIFF PIKE
Who?

CRANE
The man you're looking for.

She moves to the bar. Stands two stools down.

SHERIFF PIKE
That so?

CRANE
Ask the barkeep.

She looks. Behind the bar a man slumps on the floor, hands tied, mouth gagged. The BARKEEP, 50s. His eyes plead.

She keeps her face still.

SHERIFF PIKE
Untie him.

CRANE
Don't think so.

SHERIFF PIKE
I wasn't asking.

He turns, finally. Pale eyes. A thin smile.

CRANE
Augusta. We both know who's in the back room.

Her hand tightens on the holster.

SHERIFF PIKE
Say it.

CRANE
Your brother.

She says nothing. The storm howls against the shutters. A floorboard groans overhead.

CRANE (CONT'D)
He wants to talk.

SHERIFF PIKE
Does he.

CRANE
Before the hanging.

She draws. So does he. Both guns level. Neither fires. The barkeep whimpers.

From the back stair, a voice, rough and tired.

JONAH (O.S.)
Gus. Put it down.

She doesn't. Crane doesn't. The piano, somewhere, plays the same bar again.

Then she lowers the gun. Crane smiles. She turns to the stair.

SHERIFF PIKE
(softly)
Jonah.

The stair creaks. JONAH PIKE, 40s, her brother, descends out of the dark. Shackles on his wrists. A gaunt, unshaven man.
